    {
      "id": "c32dedf9ea0c5b4a7825cfd756112d6881e75b2f",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 536854992,
          "type": null,
          "body": "It looks like I missed something, `#include <QtCore/qvariant.h>` on macOS also searches in the `Headers` directory of the `QtCore` framework (`/usr/local/opt/qt5/lib/QtCore.framework/Headers/qvariant.h`). So that is how it normally finds headers by default. Other include directories (via `-isystem`, `-I`) however seem to take precedence over Framework headers.\n\nBecause some libraries (such as Libgcrypt) are found in `/usr/local/include`, that causes the issue which I am trying to fix here. Minimal reproducer:\n\n```sh\nrm -rf build && cmake -S . -B build -GNinja && ninja -vC build good && ninja -vC build bad\n```\n\nCorresponding `CMakeLists.txt`:\n```cmake\ncmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.4..3.12)\nproject(Dummy LANGUAGES CXX)\nfind_package(Qt5 COMPONENTS Core REQUIRED PATHS /usr/local/opt/qt5)\nfile(WRITE \"${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/sample.cpp\" \"#include <QAbstractTableModel>\")\n\nadd_library(good sample.cpp)\ntarget_link_libraries(good Qt5::Core)\n\nadd_library(bad sample.cpp)\ntarget_link_libraries(bad Qt5::Core)\ntarget_include_directories(bad SYSTEM PRIVATE /usr/local/include)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n# ninja -v good\n[1/2] /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/c++ -DQT_CORE_LIB -DQT_NO_DEBUG -iframework /usr/local/opt/qt5/lib -isystem /usr/local/opt/qt5/lib/QtCore.framework/Headers -isystem /usr/local/opt/qt5/./mkspecs/macx-clang -isysroot /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/SDKs/MacOSX11.1.sdk -fPIC -std=gnu++11 -MD -MT CMakeFiles/good.dir/sample.cpp.o -MF CMakeFiles/good.dir/sample.cpp.o.d -o CMakeFiles/good.dir/sample.cpp.o -c sample.cpp\n...\n# ninja -v bad\n[1/2] /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/c++ -DQT_CORE_LIB -DQT_NO_DEBUG -isystem /usr/local/include -iframework /usr/local/opt/qt5/lib -isystem /usr/local/opt/qt5/lib/QtCore.framework/Headers -isystem /usr/local/opt/qt5/./mkspecs/macx-clang -isysroot /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/SDKs/MacOSX11.1.sdk -fPIC -std=gnu++11 -MD -MT CMakeFiles/bad.dir/sample.cpp.o -MF CMakeFiles/bad.dir/sample.cpp.o.d -o CMakeFiles/bad.dir/sample.cpp.o -c sample.cpp\nFAILED: CMakeFiles/bad.dir/sample.cpp.o\n...\nIn file included from sample.cpp:1:\nIn file included from /usr/local/opt/qt5/lib/QtCore.framework/Headers/QAbstractTableModel:1:\nIn file included from /usr/local/opt/qt5/lib/QtCore.framework/Headers/qabstractitemmodel.h:43:\nIn file included from /usr/local/include/QtCore/qvariant.h:43:\nIn file included from /usr/local/include/QtCore/qatomic.h:41:\n/usr/local/include/QtCore/qglobal.h:667:26: error: no template named 'enable_if_t' in namespace 'std'; did you mean 'enable_if'?\n```\n\nUnless we can avoid including `/usr/local/include` for anything, we need this workaround I think. I can try to update the commit message/comments to mention this detail?",
          "author": {
            "id": 2415432,
            "username": "Lekensteyn",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Peter Wu",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/efcf30db53f30b15e7c905ef37bd4dc44d87573bd92baf97cd707115d3fcd0c0?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/Lekensteyn"
          },
